  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe provided solution achieves a bound of approximately 0.380887 for the Erd\u0151s minimax overlap constant. We aim to improve this bound by employing an improved particle-swarm-based optimization algorithm called Accelerated Particle Swarm Optimization Global Variable Change (APS OGV) specifically adapted for finite-precision representation. This approach uses course-to-fine resolution strategy, multi-stage parameter adjustment, and stratified population management to escape local optima. Unlike the original method, it leverages the precision-success tradeoff and adaptive dimensionality reduction to explore better configurations without suffering dimensionality curse.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation: Function approximated by piecewise linear segments with adjustable number of knots at 32 equally spaced points across [0,2]\n2. Variables: Control points h_k for k=1 to n_points (400 by default) constrained to [0,1] and sum limited by 1-dx (i.e., sum(h)=n_points/2)\n3. Objective: $$ \\min_{h} \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx $$\n   Transformed via discretization: maximize min_over_k cross-correlation (followed by dx adjustment)\n4. Dimensions: n=401\u219232 control points (via B-spline approximation)\n5. Constraints: \n   - 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1.0 for all i\n   - sum(h) (dx)=1.0 with dx=2.0/n\n6. Formulation: Particle swarm with adaptive precision control (higher precision for potentially promising dimensions) and global variable change strategy\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: seed, budget_s, initial_h_values\n2. Initialization:\n   a. n_base=400, n_inters=8 (number of intermediate control points)\n   b. h_base = initial_h_values.copy()\n   c. Scale constraint: target_sum = (n_base*dx)//1\n3. Course-to-fine resolution:\n   i. Start with coarse grid (32 control points)\n   ii. Optimize control points via APS OGV algorithm\n   iii. Refine to more points if improvement threshold met\n4. APS OGV algorithm:\n   a. Population initialization: m_ball=20 particles/city/dimension\n   b. Dimension priority: 2 balls per dimension\n   c. Variable change interval: 100 iterations per change\n   d. Precision management: 16-48 bits per dimension, adaptive switching\n5. Objective calculation:\n   i. Compute sample x: np.linspace(0,2,n_base)\n   ii. For each particle, compute function via control points\n   iii. Compute raw correlation with np.correlate(..., mode='full')\n   iv. Scale with dx=2.0/n_base\n6. Data management:\n   a. Track best solution and fitness at all times\n   b. Regular feasibility restoration via projection\n   c. Use priority strategy for dimension investment\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Primary variation: Progressive precision (bits=16\u219248 per dimension)\n2. Secondary variation: Adaptive dimension investment (based on fitness gradient)\n3. Termination variations:\n   - Early exit if success threshold 0.36 reached\n   - Performance monitoring for local optimum detection\n   - Variable change frequency increase as progress slows\n4. Population management:\n   - 2 elite solutions per change\n   - 90% population reuse with 10% renewal\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n1. Time estimation: \n   - Apex compute: 500\u00d720\u00d720\u00d740 = 80000 evaluations (at maximum precision)\n   - Budget scaling: 1300\u219220000 evaluations for maximal search\n   - Projected solution quality: threshold for 0.36 bound requires ~3000 evaluations\n2. Feasibility:\n   - Integrated projection after every evaluation\n   - Adaptive variable selection in precision change to mitigate crossings\n   - Hierarchical retry mechanism for invalid/overflow\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Final validation:\n   - Re-evaluate using compute_c5 function as given\n   - Check sum(h*(1-h(x+k))) correlation\n   - Verify sum(h) equals n_points/2\n   - Check all values between 0 and 1\n2. Output contract: \n   - h_values: Final control points (length n_points)\n   - c5_bound: Optimized constant estimate\n   - n_points: Explicitly specified for stability\n3. Final steps before return:\n   - Run single-error to-final-check\n   - Override precision to 32 bits for final evaluation\n   - Ensure strict adherence to constraints before reporting",
